%S A399019 0,12,78,90,276,240,594,462,1032,756,1590,1122,2268,1560,3066,2070,
%T A399019 3984,2652,5022,3306,6180,4032,7458,4830,8856,5700,10374,6642,12012,
%U A399019 7656,13770,8742,15648,9900,17646,11130,19764,12432,22002,13806,24360,15252,26838,16770
%N A399019 Number of empty unit triangles in the hexagonal cross-section of the first iteration of the generalized Menger sponge with subdivision 2n+1, cut through the center perpendicular to a space diagonal.
%C A399019 The sponge: subdivide a cube into (2n+1)^3 equal cells, number the coordinates 0..2n along each axis, and keep a cell when at most one of its three coordinates is odd. n = 1 gives the first iteration of the classical Menger sponge, with A395241(1) = 7 cells removed.
%C A399019 The cut: the plane through the center perpendicular to a space diagonal meets the cube in a regular hexagon of side 2n+1, tiled by 6*(2n+1)^2 unit triangles of a triangular lattice. a(n) counts the triangles lying in removed cells and A399018(n) counts the rest.
%C A399019 a(n) is always divisible by 6, by the 6-fold rotational symmetry of the cross-section about its center; that center is a lattice vertex rather than the centroid of a triangle, so no triangle is fixed and they fall into orbits of size 6. Hence a(n)/6 = 0, 2, 13, 15, 46, 40, 99, 77, 172, 126, ... counts the empty triangles of one 60-degree sextant.
%C A399019 The central cell (n, n, n) is removed when n is odd and kept when n is even, so the unit hexagon at the center of the cross-section alternates solid, empty, solid, ...; this parity is the source of the period-2 split in the formulas below.
%C A399019 a(1) = 12: the twelve empty triangles form a single hexagram, a six-pointed star. Iterating the sponge fills the cross-section with hexagrams of every size, each new one costing exactly 12 unit triangles; see A299916.
%H A399019 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="/A399019/b399019.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A399019 Paul Bourke, <a href="https://paulbourke.net/fractals/mrlymath/">mrly fractals: Menger, Sierpinski, Cantor</a>.
%H A399019 George Hart, <a href="https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2012/12/10/mathematical-impressions-the-surprising-menger-sponge-slice/">Mathematical Impressions: The Surprising Menger Sponge Slice</a>, Simons Foundation, 2012.
%H A399019 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="https://paulbourke.net/fractals/mrlymath/mrlymath.pdf">Marley Math: Cantor Sets, Sierpinski Carpets, Menger Sponges, And More</a>.
%H A399019 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="https://github.com/carlomitchener/carlomitchener/blob/main/some26/README.md">Generalized Menger Sponge Slice</a>, 2026.
%H A399019 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="/A399019/a399019.png">Illustration of a(1), a(2), a(3): the empty triangles, in black</a>.
%H A399019 <a href="/index/Rec#order_06">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (0,3,0,-3,0,1).
%F A399019 a(n) = 3*n*(5*n+3) for n even; a(n) = 3*n*(3*n+1) for n odd.
%F A399019 a(n) = 3*n*(4*n + 2 + (n+1)*(-1)^n).
%F A399019 a(n) = 3*a(n-2) - 3*a(n-4) + a(n-6) for n > 5.
%F A399019 G.f.: 6*x*(1 + 6*x + x^2)*(2 + x + x^2)/((1-x)^3*(1+x)^3).
%F A399019 a(n) = 6*(2*n+1)^2 - A399018(n).
%F A399019 a(2*n+1) = 6*A033568(n+1); a(2*n) = 6*n*(10*n+3).
%F A399019 A399018(n) - a(n) = 6*(2*n+1) - 6*n*(n+1)*(-1)^n.
%F A399019 a(2*n+1) = A399018(2*n) + 6*(2*n+1); a(2*n) = A399018(2*n-1) + 36*n for n >= 1.
%F A399019 E.g.f.: 3*x*((4 + 5*x)*cosh(x) + (8 + 3*x)*sinh(x)). - _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 16 2026
%e A399019 a(0) = 0: no cell is removed, so the cross-section is a solid hexagon with no holes.
%e A399019 a(1) = 12: of the 6*3^2 = 54 unit triangles in the cross-section of the first Menger sponge iteration, 12 are empty, and they form one six-pointed star.
%t A399019 A399019[n_] := 3*n*(4*n + (-1)^n*(n + 1) + 2); Array[A399019, 50, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Aug 27 2026 *)
%o A399019 (Python)
%o A399019 def a(n): return 3*n*(4*n + 2 + (n + 1)*(-1)**n)
%o A399019 (Python)
%o A399019 from itertools import product
%o A399019 def a(n):
%o A399019     m = 2*n + 1
%o A399019     c = 3*(m - 1)//2
%o A399019     w = {c - 1: 1, c: 6, c + 1: 1}
%o A399019     return sum(w[s] for x, y, z in product(range(m), repeat=3) if (s := x + y + z) in w and (x%2) + (y%2) + (z%2) > 1)
%Y A399019 Cf. A399018 (the solid triangles of the same cross-section).
%Y A399019 Cf. A299916 (star-shaped holes in the cross-section of the classical Menger sponge).
%Y A399019 Cf. A103532, A395241 (cells kept and cells removed by the same sponge).
%Y A399019 Cf. A033568 (the odd bisection of a(n)/6), A195018 (= n*(10*n-3), the analogous odd bisection for A399018).
%K A399019 nonn,easy,changed
%O A399019 0,2
%A A399019 _Carlo Mitchener_, Aug 16 2026
